# Typography

Set all Civica text in Roboto, at 2 weights: regular and bold. Take every size from the type scale. Then a room name, a reading and a heading look the same on every dashboard.

Roboto is the typeface Home Assistant already uses on its own screens, so a dashboard never has to download a font.

## Text in each context

This table shows the typeface and the smallest text to use in each place Civica text appears.

| Context | Typeface | Smallest text | Who controls it |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Civica card or panel on a dashboard | Roboto | 16px supporting text | The Civica theme |
| Native Home Assistant screen | Roboto | 12px compact extra text | Home Assistant, with Civica sizes |
| Chart label or legend | Roboto | 14px | The Civica chart cards |
| Companion app settings, keyboard and share sheet | The phone's own font | Set by the phone | The phone's operating system |
| Printed household handover | Roboto | 12pt body text | The person printing it |
| Social image or release picture | Roboto | Large enough to read on a phone | The person making it |

Every screen size in this table grows with Home Assistant's font size scale and with browser or app zoom.

This chart compares the smallest screen text in each context. 3 contexts have no bar. Their smallest size is set by the phone, measured in points on paper, or not fixed. None of them is 0.

<%=
  hex = ->(id) { govuk_colour(id).fetch('hex') }
  ink, grid, blue = hex.('black'), hex.('black-tint-80'), hex.('blue')
  contexts = [
    ['civica card or panel', 16, nil],
    ['Native Home Assistant screen', 12, nil],
    ['Chart label or legend', 14, nil],
    ['Companion app settings', nil, 'Set by the phone'],
    ['Printed household handover', nil, '12pt on paper, not on this scale'],
    ['Social image or release picture', nil, 'Not fixed: large enough to read on a phone']
  ]
  x_of = ->(px) { 4 + px * 12 }
  svg = []
  [0, 4, 8, 12, 16].each { |v| svg << %(<line x1="#{x_of.(v)}" y1="4" x2="#{x_of.(v)}" y2="208" stroke="#{grid}" stroke-width="1"/><text x="#{x_of.(v)}" y="222" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">#{v}</text>) }
  svg << %(<text x="#{x_of.(16)}" y="236" font-size="10" text-anchor="middle" fill="#{ink}">Smallest text (px)</text>)
  contexts.each_with_index do |(name, px, note), i|
    y = 16 + i * 33
    svg << %(<text x="4" y="#{y}" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#{ink}">#{name}</text>)
    svg << (px ? %(<rect x="4" y="#{y + 5}" width="#{px * 12}" height="12" fill="#{blue}"/><text x="#{x_of.(px) + 5}" y="#{y + 15}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">#{px}px</text>) : %(<text x="6" y="#{y + 15}" font-size="10" fill="#{ink}">No bar. #{note}</text>))
  end
  specimen = contexts.select { |c| c[1] }.map { |name, px, _| %(<p style="font-family:Roboto,Helvetica Neue,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;font-size:#{px}px;line-height:1.5;margin:0 0 5px">Living room 21.4°C, updated 14:05 <span style="font-size:16px">(#{name}, #{px}px)</span></p>) }.join
  %(<div class="c-colour-example"><figure style="margin:0 0 20px">) +
    %(<svg role="img" aria-labelledby="context-sizes-title context-sizes-desc" viewBox="0 0 320 240" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:480px;height:auto;font-family:Roboto,Helvetica Neue,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif"><title id="context-sizes-title">Bar chart of the smallest screen text in each context</title><desc id="context-sizes-desc">A civica card or panel has the largest smallest text, 16px. A chart label or legend is 14px, and a native Home Assistant screen 12px. The Companion app settings have no bar because the phone sets the size. The printed handover has no bar because it is measured in points on paper: 12pt. A social image has no bar because its size is not fixed. The table above gives the same facts.</desc><g aria-hidden="true">#{svg.join}</g></svg>) +
    %(<p style="margin:20px 0 10px">The same reading at each fixed screen size:</p>#{specimen}) +
    %(<figcaption>Smallest text in each context, before the font size scale and zoom. The table above gives the same facts.</figcaption></figure></div>)
%>

